    "This is a great-tailed grackle! The male is shiny black with a very long tail and yellow eyes. The smaller female is brown. Grackles gather in noisy groups around trees and open spaces, even in parking lots!": "a63ccf66c1cc.mp3",
    "This is a green anole! A green anole is a little lizard that can turn green or brown. It climbs using sticky toe pads. Males show a pink throat fan called a dewlap to signal to other anoles.": "d27a0561dd17.mp3",
    "This is a gulf coast toad! This toad has bumpy skin and short back legs for hopping. It comes out to look for food when the air is damp. Like frogs, toads begin as tadpoles in water. In a garden, they help by eating insects.": "c7b55494240f.mp3",
    "This is a kemp’s ridley sea turtle! Kemp’s ridley sea turtles live in the Gulf of Mexico. A nesting mother comes ashore to bury eggs in the sand. This is a rare sight! If you find a sea turtle on a real beach, watch from far away and tell a park ranger.": "db2ef5ac1280.mp3",
    "This is a killdeer! Two dark bands cross a killdeer’s white chest. Its name sounds like its loud kill-deer call! A parent may pretend to have a broken wing to draw danger away from its chicks.": "3e2b2e5d1257.mp3",
    "This is a laughing gull! A laughing gull’s ha-ha-ha call sounds like laughter! Adults have a black head during nesting season. In winter, the dark hood fades. Look for these gray-and-white birds along the Texas coast.": "ecb4a4fa71cc.mp3",
    "This is a least tern! This tiny tern hovers over water, then dives to catch a small fish. It has a yellow bill and a white forehead. Least terns nest on open sand in the warmer months. Watch from outside marked nesting areas.": "276b3cca0e44.mp3",
    "This is a monarch butterfly! Monarchs have orange wings with black veins and white dots. They travel through Texas in spring and fall. Monarch caterpillars eat milkweed leaves. Planting milkweed and native flowers helps butterflies.": "e0ff08fd91a6.mp3",
    "This is a mourning dove! Mourning doves make soft cooing calls. Their long, pointed tails help you tell them from other backyard birds. They pick seeds from the ground. Their wings can whistle as they take off.": "4e676c447714.mp3",
    "This is a nine-banded armadillo! An armadillo wears a bony shell like a suit of armor. It digs in the soil to find insects and grubs. Its strong claws dig burrows where it can rest. It uses its nose to sniff out food.": "4f6f311e7548.mp3",
    "This is a north american river otter! An otter has webbed feet and a long, strong tail that help it swim. It is a playful wetland mammal! Otters can slide down muddy banks. Healthy streams and wetlands give them places to find food.": "1a44c0c2c5f5.mp3",
    "This is a northern cardinal! Male cardinals are bright red. Females are mostly brown with red accents. Both have a pointed head crest! Their strong beaks crack seeds. Listen for whistles from shrubs and small trees.": "cfba3323b439.mp3",
    "This is a northern cottonmouth! A cottonmouth is a venomous snake of wetlands. Its name comes from the pale inside of its mouth. Watch every snake from far away with a grown-up. Color alone cannot tell you whether a snake is safe.": "4b97edaa8253.mp3",
    "This is a northern mockingbird! The northern mockingbird is the Texas state bird. It copies other birds’ songs and even human-made sounds! When it flies, look for bright white patches on its gray wings.": "988dd02a6990.mp3",
    "This is a nutria! Nutria are big water-loving rodents with orange front teeth and round, skinny tails. They came to Texas from South America. Their feeding and digging can damage wetland plants.": "354f472a3cd0.mp3",
    "This is a piping plover! A piping plover runs, stops, and picks tiny animals from the sand. Its pale feathers blend into the beach. These small shorebirds visit the Texas coast outside their northern breeding season, including winter.": "8bd6aa6a5a79.mp3",
    "This is a plain-bellied watersnake! This nonvenomous watersnake lives near ponds, lakes, and rivers. Its belly has very little pattern. It can swim well and also travel on land. Give all wild snakes space and never try to pick one up.": "e4f5c1412215.mp3",
    "This is a raccoon! Look for a black face mask and a tail with dark rings! Raccoons often explore after dark. Their front paws are very sensitive and help them feel for food, even in muddy water.": "1947d703fc9f.mp3",
    "This is a red-bellied woodpecker! This woodpecker has a black-and-white barred back and red on its head. Its reddish belly can be hard to see! It taps on trees and uses a long tongue to find insects. It also eats fruit and nuts.": "6ab83a4bcae6.mp3",
    "This is a red-eared slider! Look for the red stripe behind each eye! These freshwater turtles love to bask together on logs. A sunny log warms a turtle’s body. If it is startled, it slides into the water.": "838faf9f63f0.mp3",
    "This is a red-tailed hawk! A red-tailed hawk watches open fields for mice and other small animals. Adults usually have a rusty-red tail. It can soar in circles on rising warm air. Young hawks have striped tails instead of red ones.": "adc0bb9721f6.mp3",
    "This is a red-winged blackbird! The male has bright red shoulder patches edged in yellow. The female is streaky brown. Listen near cattails for its loud song. Many blackbirds gather in flocks outside nesting season.": "2f190960e8a5.mp3",
    "This is a roseate spoonbill! A roseate spoonbill is a pink bird with a bill shaped like a big, flat spoon. It swishes its spoon side to side in the water to feel for food. Spoonbills get their pink color from the tiny shrimp and crabs they eat. Roseate means rosy pink!": "94fbd09fb42a.mp3",
    "This is a ruby-throated hummingbird! This tiny bird can hover beside flowers! Males have a ruby-red throat that flashes in the light. Look for migrating hummingbirds in spring and fall. Nectar gives them energy, and insects help them grow.": "7ec4f1bb6e25.mp3",
    "This is a scissor-tailed flycatcher! This bird has an amazing long, forked tail! It catches flying insects over open fields. Look for it on fences and wires during the warmer months. Its tail opens like scissors when it turns.": "531e529ee463.mp3",
    "This is a southern leopard frog! The dark spots on this frog look a little like a leopard’s spots. Long back legs help it leap. Webbed back feet help it swim away through the water.": "ba1e578ce969.mp3",
    "This is a three-toed box turtle! A hinge in this turtle’s shell lets it close up for protection, a little like a box with a lid! It lives on land in damp woods. Its back feet usually have three toes.": "2a9fafbcee2c.mp3",
    "This is a virginia opossum! An opossum is a marsupial. Its tiny babies grow inside their mother’s pouch! Older babies ride on their mother’s back. An opossum has a pale face and a long, nearly bare tail.": "3ac7417ebb86.mp3",
    "This is a white ibis! A white ibis has a long, curved orange bill. It pokes its bill into the mud to feel for crawfish. White ibises walk around in big, busy groups. Look for them in ponds, ditches, and even front yards after it rains!": "67642426e286.mp3",
    "This is a white-tailed deer! White-tailed deer are the most common deer in Texas. When a deer is scared, it lifts its tail like a white flag to warn the others. Only boy deer, called bucks, grow antlers. Their antlers fall off every winter, and new ones grow in the spring! Baby deer, called fawns, have white spots to help them hide.": "a5f6062e2b73.mp3",
    "This is a wild hog! Wild hogs are pigs that went wild. They dig up the ground with their strong snouts, looking for roots, bugs, and worms. Pigs came to Texas from far away, long ago. Now millions of wild hogs live here, and their digging makes big messes. Wild hogs can be grumpy, so always watch them from far away.": "c415c73dc184.mp3",
    "This is a wood duck! Wood ducks nest in holes in trees near wooded wetlands. The male wears bright colors and a swept-back crest. Ducklings jump from their tree nest and follow their mother to the water!": "6df5eccc71df.mp3",
    "This is an American alligator! Alligators live in bayous, ponds, and lakes all around Katy. An alligator can hide in the water with just its eyes and nose poking out. Baby alligators chirp to call their mom, and she helps them get to the water. Alligators are wild, so always stay far away, and never, ever feed one!": "3ea6dc04d381.mp3",
    "This is an american avocet! An avocet has a bill that curves upward! It sweeps the bill through shallow water to find tiny animals. Its head turns rusty orange in breeding season and gray in winter. Look around coastal lagoons.": "c4b2f5530632.mp3",
    "This is an american bullfrog! Listen for a deep jug-o-rum call! Bullfrogs are big frogs that live beside ponds and slow water. Tadpoles breathe in water. As they grow, they sprout legs and become frogs.": "af8019eababa.mp3",
    "This is an american kestrel! This small falcon can hover into the wind while looking for a meal. Look for two dark stripes on its face. It often watches fields from a fence or wire, then swoops down to catch insects or small animals.": "c500c1fbdab7.mp3",
    "This is an atlantic ghost crab! Ghost crabs are pale, speedy crabs that live in burrows on sandy beaches. They can run sideways! Their eyes sit on stalks. Look near burrow openings and leave their sandy homes undisturbed.": "2f85efd80a39.mp3",
    "This is an eastern cottontail! White fur under this rabbit’s tail looks like a little cotton ball. Big ears help it listen for danger. Cottontails nibble plants near places where they can hide quickly in thick grass or brush.": "3eb6160db920.mp3",
    "This is an eastern fox squirrel! Fox squirrels have bushy tails and often a rusty-colored belly. They bury nuts and find some again by smell. A forgotten acorn can grow into a new oak tree. Squirrels help plant the forest!": "9a47abd699a8.mp3",
    "This is an eastern meadowlark! An eastern meadowlark has a bright yellow chest with a black V, like a necklace. Listen for its clear whistles! It hides its nest on the ground among grasses. Healthy prairie gives it a place to raise chicks.": "697d944c541d.mp3",
    "Three-toed box turtle! A hinge in this turtle’s shell lets it close up for protection, a little like a box with a lid!": "b4e1510ac97b.mp3",
    "Three-toed box turtle! It eats insects, worms, mushrooms, and fruit.": "26ef9c58944e.mp3",
    "Three-toed box turtle! It lives on land in damp woods. Its back feet usually have three toes.": "98784e459f09.mp3",
    "Virginia opossum! An opossum is a marsupial. Its tiny babies grow inside their mother’s pouch!": "0cd448c8e5f0.mp3",
    "Virginia opossum! It eats insects, fruit, eggs, and many other foods.": "66be52426ab6.mp3",
    "Virginia opossum! Older babies ride on their mother’s back. An opossum has a pale face and a long, nearly bare tail.": "11782a05722c.mp3",
